    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok that makes sense. can u go into more detail about what happens with the aldosterone part though? like what exactly does the drop in angiotensin ii do to the aldosterone levels and how does that affect sodium/potassium stuff?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Since Angiotensin II normally stimulates the release of aldosterone from the adrenal glands, the reduction in Angiotensin II causes a decrease in aldosterone secretion. Aldosterone's primary role is to promote sodium ($\\text{Na}^+$) reabsorption and potassium ($\\text{K}^+$) secretion in the kidneys. With lower aldosterone levels, less sodium is retained by the body, and more potassium is preserved, leading to a mild potassium-sparing effect.",
      "source": "target",
