    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so basically it stops the downstream effects too. can u tie it all together for me? like walk me through the whole chain from renin being released to the actual blood pressure drop so i can visualize the full process for the exam.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When blood pressure drops, the kidney releases renin, initiating the RAAS cascade. Renin converts angiotensinogen to Angiotensin I, which ACE then turns into Angiotensin II. Lisinopril blocks ACE, preventing Angiotensin II formation, which consequently lowers aldosterone release. The resulting decrease in Angiotensin II causes vasodilation and reduced sodium/water retention, ultimately leading to a measurable drop in blood pressure.",
      "source": "target",
